  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1254 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nA weak disturbance over the Intermountain West will approach the\nhigh plains this afternoon. Abundant low level moisture over\nnorth Texas and southwest Oklahoma is forecast to surge\nnorthward today ahead of a dry line as a warm front lifts north.\nThe intersection of the dry line and front will be a favored\narea for t-storms with daytime heating. 0-6 km bulk shear is\nmarginally favorable for supercells with very large hail.\nHowever, the upper level shear (500-200mb) is quite weak so that\nupdrafts will tend to collapse quickly, precluding long-lived\nsupercells with very large hail. There will be a large hail \nconcern initially; but very warm temperatures at mid to upper \nlevels (500mb temperature of -4C) and the weak upper level shear\nwill tend to limit hail size, with storms transitioning to \noutflow dominated and linear structures. Damaging wind gusts \nare the main concern. Upscale growth into a larger storm cluster\nis likely during the evening, which is the reasoning behind the\nforecasted high rain chances, especially from Dodge City \nnorthward to I-70. A heat advisory was issued for locations\nsouth of Dodge City for today as dew point temperatures in the\nupper 60s combine with actual temperatures from 100 to 103 to\nyield heat indices from 105 to 108.\n\nAn upper level ridge will gradually build across the high \nplains over the weekend, resulting in a warming trend and dry \nweather. The various ensemble means indicate a likelihood \nfor temperatures between 105 and 110 degrees. Temperatures from\nthe national blend of models (NBM), which often struggles \nduring extreme events, is several degrees too cool for this heat\nwave and only shows 102 for Sunday and 103 for Monday. The \nintense heat will persist through Tuesday before the upper level\nridge becomes suppressed southward once again, allowing a weak \ncold front to pass. But temperatures may reach 100 degrees even \non Wednesday and Thursday from Dodge City southward. One\npositive is that the deep vertical mixing that helps create the\nheat will also tend to dilute the low level moisture in the \nvertical, resulting in heat indices that are equal to or a \nlittle below the actual temperature. However, look for the\nmoisture to increase as the front passes, which may keep heat\nindices on the high side. There are small chances for t-storms \nby late next week as the front arrives; but any significant \nrains will be isolated.",
